    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A turbo nozzle and a surface cleaner both promise cleaner concrete faster than a standard tip, but they do it in opposite ways. The turbo nozzle concentrates force into a spinning pencil jet that cuts through stains. The surface cleaner spreads that force across a wide, enclosed path for an even finish. For a whole driveway, the surface cleaner wins on evenness and speed. For stubborn spots, edges and anything that is not flat, the turbo nozzle does what a deck cannot. Most owners who clean concrete regularly end up using both.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="how" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How a Turbo Nozzle and a Surface Cleaner Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            A turbo nozzle, also sold as a rotary or rotating nozzle, fits on the end of your wand like any other quick-connect tip. Inside is a small 0-degree jet mounted in a cone that spins as water passes through it. The result is a pencil-thin, high-impact stream that traces a circle many times per second, giving roughly the cutting power of a 0-degree tip with a wider effective footprint.
          </p>

          <p>
            A surface cleaner is a separate attachment, usually a 12 to 20 inch round deck on casters with a spray bar inside. The bar carries two or four nozzles, typically 15-degree or similar fan tips, angled so the water spins the bar. The deck holds the nozzles at a fixed height and the skirt contains the spray, so every part of the path gets swept the same way.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fundamental difference is control. With a turbo nozzle, you control the distance, angle and dwell time by hand on every inch of the surface. With a surface cleaner, the deck controls distance and angle for you, and you only control speed and overlap. That is why one is precise but uneven, and the other is even but less aggressive on any single spot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both depend on your washer. A turbo nozzle must be sized to your machine's PSI and GPM, usually by an orifice number on the tip. A surface cleaner's bar speed depends on flow, as covered in our guide to <a href="/what-size-surface-cleaner-do-i-need/">sizing a surface cleaner</a>. Either attachment performs poorly on a washer it was not built for.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
