                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Turbo Nozzle</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface Cleaner</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Attaches to</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">End of wand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gun or wand, replaces it</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spray</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spinning 0-degree jet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two to four fan jets on a spinning bar</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Distance control</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">By hand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fixed by deck</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spray containment</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Skirt contains most spray</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Best on</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stains, edges, vertical surfaces</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large flat areas</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="even" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Evenness and Finish
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the surface cleaner's biggest advantage. Because the deck keeps every nozzle at the same height and the bar sweeps each spot many times, a slab cleaned with a well-matched surface cleaner dries to an even tone. The overlapping arcs blend together into a uniform finish rather than a set of visible passes.
          </p>

          <p>
            A turbo nozzle relies on your hand. Hold it a few inches closer for a moment and that spot gets cleaned harder; move faster across another area and it gets cleaned less. On concrete, those differences show up as wand marks, light and dark stripes or circular blotches once the slab dries. Skilled operators reduce them, but they are hard to eliminate across a whole driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            The surface cleaner is not immune to stripes. Moving too fast, skipping overlap or using a deck too big for the washer all leave patterns, as covered in our guide to <a href="/surface-cleaner-leaving-streaks/">surface cleaner streaks</a>. But when used correctly, it produces a far more consistent result than any handheld tip on large flat areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where evenness matters less, such as cleaning a stained spot before the overall wash, or cleaning a wall, step riser or fence, the turbo nozzle's unevenness is not a problem. It is only on wide, visible flatwork that the difference really shows.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan to seal the concrete afterwards, evenness matters even more. Sealer tends to highlight differences in surface tone, and wand stripes can remain visible under a clear coat. Our guide to <a href="/pressure-wash-driveway-before-sealing/">pressure washing before sealing</a> covers why a uniform clean is the starting point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Speed and Coverage
          </h2>

          <p>
            On open flatwork, a surface cleaner is usually much faster. A 15 or 16 inch deck cleans a lane well over a foot wide in a single pass, and you walk it in straight lines with generous overlap. A turbo nozzle's effective cleaning footprint is much smaller, and you have to work it back and forth over each section to get it clean.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manufacturers and contractors commonly describe surface cleaners as cutting large flatwork jobs to a fraction of the time a wand takes. The exact saving depends on the deck size, your washer and how dirty the surface is, but on a full driveway it is often substantial. The deck also reduces fatigue, since you push a rolling tool rather than holding a wand at an angle for hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            The picture reverses on small, broken-up areas. A turbo nozzle needs no setup and can clean a single oil stain, a set of steps or a narrow walk in minutes. Swapping a surface cleaner on and off for a small job takes longer than just doing it with the nozzle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinsing is the hidden time cost of both. A surface cleaner leaves loosened dirt in a slurry on the slab that needs a rinse at the end. A turbo nozzle throws dirt and water outward and can spatter walls, cars and windows, which sometimes need cleaning afterwards. Plan time for cleanup either way.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="damage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Damage Risk to Concrete, Pavers and Wood
          </h2>

          <p>
            A turbo nozzle is the more dangerous tool for surfaces. Its spinning 0-degree jet carries a lot of energy into a tiny area, and on concrete it can etch the surface, expose aggregate or leave visible tracks if held too close or too long. On wood, softer stone, vinyl and painted surfaces it can do serious damage in seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            A surface cleaner spreads its energy across wider fan jets at a fixed height, so it is much harder to gouge a surface. That does not make it harmless. On pavers it can still blow out joint sand, and on soft or weathered concrete it can lift the top layer if you linger. But for general flatwork it is the safer of the two.
          </p>

          <p>
            The jets on both tools can injure skin. A turbo nozzle's stream is especially dangerous and should never be pointed at people, pets or your own feet. A surface cleaner's jets are enclosed, but they are still strong enough to cut skin if you tip the deck or reach under it while it is running.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our guide to <a href="/what-not-to-pressure-wash/">what not to pressure wash</a> lists the surfaces where neither tool should be used at full power, and our explainer on <a href="/pressure-washer-nozzle-colors-explained/">nozzle colours</a> covers which standard tips are gentler alternatives for delicate jobs.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Treat a turbo nozzle like a 0-degree tip.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Keep it moving, keep it at a distance and test on a hidden spot first. It is a stain-removal tool, not a whole-driveway tool.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="where" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where Each Tool Wins
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use a surface cleaner for large, flat horizontal surfaces where an even finish matters: driveways, garage aprons, sidewalks, pool decks and concrete patios. It is also the better choice when cleaning ahead of sealing, since an uneven clean shows through the sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a turbo nozzle for heavy stains and stubborn spots that the surface cleaner cannot lift in one pass, such as old oil, rust, tyre marks and thick moss. It also handles areas a round deck cannot reach: corners, edges against walls, steps, curbs and the space around posts and drains.
          </p>

          <p>
            For pavers and natural stone, the surface cleaner at reduced pressure is usually the better choice for overall cleaning, because the turbo nozzle's concentrated jet is more likely to damage the surface or strip joints. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-pressure-wash-a-patio/">pressure washing a patio</a> covers material-specific settings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vertical surfaces are a turbo nozzle job, but only on durable masonry. A surface cleaner is designed for horizontal work. For siding, fences and decks, neither is usually the right tool; a wider fan tip and a suitable cleaner work better, as covered in our guide to <a href="/how-to-pressure-wash-vinyl-siding/">pressure washing vinyl siding</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Garage floors are a special case. A surface cleaner works well on bare concrete garage floors, and its skirt keeps spray off stored items and walls. On epoxy-coated or painted floors, though, use low pressure and a mild cleaner instead of either tool, since high pressure can lift a coating at its edges or weak spots.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost and Compatibility
          </h2>

          <p>
            A turbo nozzle is the cheaper buy. Most are sold as a single quick-connect tip, and many pressure washers include one in the box. The main thing to check is the orifice size, since a turbo nozzle sized for a 4,000 PSI washer will not perform properly on a 2,000 PSI one.
          </p>

          <p>
            A surface cleaner costs more, often several times the price of a turbo nozzle, and the better decks with serviceable swivels and quality casters cost more again. Our <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">surface cleaner rankings</a> cover the range from budget universal decks to commercial attachments.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compatibility runs through both. Turbo nozzles generally use the standard 1/4-inch quick-connect, though some electric washers use proprietary tips. Surface cleaners usually use a 1/4-inch plug, while some brand attachments use bayonet or proprietary fittings. Check your wand and gun before buying either.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric washers deserve a specific note. Their lower flow can struggle to spin a large surface cleaner, so a small deck designed for electric machines is the realistic option. A turbo nozzle sized for electric washers works but delivers less impact than on a gas machine, as covered in our guide to whether an <a href="/can-an-electric-pressure-washer-clean-concrete/">electric pressure washer can clean concrete</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="both" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Using Both Together
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people who clean concrete more than once a year end up owning both, and they work best in sequence. Start by sweeping and pre-treating stains. Use the turbo nozzle to break down the worst spots, such as oil drips and rust. Then go over the whole area with the surface cleaner for an even finish that blends those spot-treated areas in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do edges and corners with the turbo nozzle or a 25-degree tip before or after the surface cleaner, feathering the spray into the deck-cleaned area so there is no visible boundary. Our <a href="/how-to-use-a-pressure-washer-surface-cleaner/">surface cleaner technique guide</a> covers edging and the final rinse.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you can only buy one, buy for the job you do most. A homeowner who cleans a whole driveway every year gets more value from a well-matched surface cleaner. Someone who mostly spot-cleans stains, steps and walls gets more from a turbo nozzle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Either way, match the tool to your washer. A turbo nozzle sized wrong or a surface cleaner too big for your flow will disappoint, and the fix is the right size rather than more effort.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store and maintain both properly. Flush a surface cleaner with clean water after each use and check its nozzles and swivel each season. Rinse a turbo nozzle and shake it to make sure the internal ball or cone moves freely; a stuck turbo nozzle sprays a fixed jet that is easy to misjudge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cleaning a whole driveway with a turbo nozzle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It can be done, but it is slow and almost always leaves visible wand marks or circular blotches once the concrete dries. Use the turbo nozzle for stains and edges and a surface cleaner for the open flatwork.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a turbo nozzle on soft surfaces</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Its concentrated jet can etch concrete and seriously damage wood, soft stone, vinyl and paint in seconds. Test on a hidden spot, keep it moving and use it only on durable masonry and stubborn stains.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a surface cleaner to lift deep stains alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A surface cleaner passes over each spot quickly and is better at even general cleaning than at deep stain removal. Pre-treat and spot-clean heavy oil and rust first, then finish with the deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying either tool without checking sizing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Turbo nozzles are sized by orifice to a washer's output, and surface cleaners are sized by deck diameter to flow. A mismatched tool underperforms. Check your washer's rated PSI and GPM before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the edges until last and rushing them</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A round deck cannot reach corners and walls. Edging with a turbo nozzle or 25-degree tip, feathered into the deck-cleaned area, prevents a visible border. Do it carefully rather than as an afterthought.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a turbo nozzle or surface cleaner better for driveways?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For cleaning a whole driveway, a surface cleaner is usually better. It is faster on open flatwork and leaves a more even finish because it holds the nozzles at a fixed height. A turbo nozzle is better for pre-treating stubborn stains and for edges and corners the deck cannot reach.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a turbo nozzle damage concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if held too close or too long. Its spinning 0-degree jet can etch the surface, expose aggregate or leave visible tracks. Keep it moving at a consistent distance, test on a hidden area first and avoid using it on soft or weathered concrete.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a surface cleaner if I have a turbo nozzle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not strictly, but it helps a lot on large flat areas. A turbo nozzle can clean a driveway, but it is slower and tends to leave uneven marks. If you clean a full driveway or patio regularly, a surface cleaner is usually worth buying alongside the nozzle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a turbo nozzle on pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use caution. The concentrated jet can damage softer pavers and strip joint sand quickly. For overall paver cleaning, a surface cleaner at reduced pressure is usually safer. Reserve the turbo nozzle for isolated stubborn stains, tested on a hidden paver first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a turbo nozzle work with an electric pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if it is sized for your washer's PSI and GPM. Turbo nozzles are sold in orifice sizes to match different machines. On an electric washer, expect less impact than on a gas machine, but it still cleans stubborn spots better than a standard fan tip.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is faster, a surface cleaner or a turbo nozzle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On large flat areas, a surface cleaner is usually much faster because it cleans a wide, even lane in each pass. On small areas such as single stains, steps or narrow walks, a turbo nozzle is faster because it needs no setup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I use for edges if I have a surface cleaner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the wand with a turbo nozzle for stubborn edge staining or a 25-degree tip for general edging. Hold it at a consistent distance and feather the spray outward into the area the surface cleaner has already done so there is no visible border.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

